WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.291
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.591 --> 00:00:09.756
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.056 --> 00:00:18.014
Unit one: Reading Literary Criticism. Today you will avoid overstatement with hedges such as may well, seems to and tends to.

4
00:00:18.314 --> 00:00:21.036
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.117
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.983 --> 00:00:27.402
Literary criticism.

7
00:00:28.417 --> 00:00:32.657
Literary criticism means the study and judgement of books, poems and plays.

8
00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:36.712
Literary criticism asks what a text really means.

9
00:00:37.317 --> 00:00:40.805
Your turn. Say: Literary criticism.

10
00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:45.318
Literary criticism.

11
00:00:46.083 --> 00:00:47.479
Critical lens.

12
00:00:48.483 --> 00:00:53.409
Critical lens means a particular way of looking at a text, such as feminist or historical.

13
00:00:54.017 --> 00:00:57.258
A historical critical lens reveals hidden politics.

14
00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:00.862
Your turn. Say: Critical lens.

15
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.096
Critical lens.

16
00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:07.478
Thematic analysis.

17
00:01:08.483 --> 00:01:12.840
Thematic analysis means the study of the main ideas that run through a work.

18
00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:16.599
Thematic analysis showed that loss unites the poems.

19
00:01:17.183 --> 00:01:20.463
Your turn. Say: Thematic analysis.

20
00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:24.882
Thematic analysis.

21
00:01:25.617 --> 00:01:26.664
Symbolism.

22
00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:31.855
Symbolism means the use of objects or images to stand for ideas.

23
00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:34.902
The storm is full of symbolism in the novel.

24
00:01:35.517 --> 00:01:38.018
Your turn. Say: Symbolism.

25
00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:41.937
Symbolism.

26
00:01:42.683 --> 00:01:43.963
Narrative technique.

27
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:48.575
Narrative technique means the method an author uses to tell a story.

28
00:01:49.183 --> 00:01:52.216
A shifting narrative technique keeps readers uncertain.

29
00:01:52.817 --> 00:01:55.713
Your turn. Say: Narrative technique.

30
00:01:58.567 --> 00:02:00.183
Narrative technique.

31
00:02:00.917 --> 00:02:02.115
Subtext.

32
00:02:03.117 --> 00:02:06.939
Subtext means the meaning beneath the words that characters actually say.

33
00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:09.805
The subtext of the dinner scene is fear.

34
00:02:10.417 --> 00:02:12.953
Your turn. Say: Subtext.

35
00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:16.859
Subtext.

36
00:02:17.617 --> 00:02:18.931
Interpretation.

37
00:02:19.917 --> 00:02:23.797
Interpretation means a reasoned explanation of what a text means.

38
00:02:24.383 --> 00:02:26.905
Her interpretation challenged the usual reading.

39
00:02:27.517 --> 00:02:30.088
Your turn. Say: Interpretation.

40
00:02:32.933 --> 00:02:34.376
Interpretation.

41
00:02:35.117 --> 00:02:35.839
Canon.

42
00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:40.707
Canon means the set of works widely accepted as the most important.

43
00:02:41.317 --> 00:02:43.781
The canon has slowly become more diverse.

44
00:02:44.383 --> 00:02:46.664
Your turn. Say: Canon.

45
00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:50.211
Canon.

46
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.906
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:55.750 --> 00:03:01.583
Visal, a critic writes 'The novel may well be read as a warning.' Why 'may well' and not simply 'is'?

48
00:03:02.683 --> 00:03:13.175
It is hedging. Critics rarely prove an interpretation, so they use may well, seems to, tends to or arguably. The claim stays reasoned but open to challenge.

49
00:03:14.283 --> 00:03:15.737
Where do the hedges go?

50
00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:29.223
Modals sit before the base verb: may well suggest. Seems to and tends to take an infinitive. Arguably usually opens the clause: Arguably, the sea symbolises freedom.

51
00:03:30.317 --> 00:03:32.096
Can a writer hedge too much?

52
00:03:33.183 --> 00:03:43.202
Yes. One hedge per claim is enough. 'It might perhaps possibly seem' sounds weak. Choose the hedge that matches the strength of your evidence.

53
00:03:44.367 --> 00:03:46.343
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:47.183 --> 00:03:52.236
Grammar focus: Hedging: may well and seems to and tends to and arguably.

55
00:03:52.536 --> 00:03:53.723
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:54.417 --> 00:03:56.660
The novel may well be read as a warning.

57
00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:00.560
The poet seems to use the sea as a symbol of freedom.

58
00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:04.158
Modern critics tend to read the ending as ironic.

59
00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:13.188
Hedge a claim with may well, seems to, tends to or arguably. It shows careful reasoning, not weakness. Use one hedge per claim.

60
00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:17.810
Be careful! Don't say: The poem is may well be a protest.

61
00:04:19.767 --> 00:04:22.649
Say: The poem may well be a protest.

62
00:04:22.949 --> 00:04:25.262
Do not add is before a modal verb.

63
00:04:26.067 --> 00:04:28.522
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:29.333 --> 00:04:30.706
Careful Claims.

65
00:04:31.006 --> 00:04:36.349
Literary criticism rarely offers proof, so careful critics hedge their claims.

66
00:04:37.333 --> 00:04:41.899
A feminist critical lens may well reveal power that a first reading misses.

67
00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:49.741
Thematic analysis tends to show that one image, such as the storm, carries several meanings through its symbolism.

68
00:04:50.733 --> 00:04:57.423
The narrator seems to hide the truth, and the subtext of each conversation is arguably more important than its words.

69
00:04:58.433 --> 00:05:02.941
Over time, a convincing interpretation can change which books are admitted to the canon.

70
00:05:04.033 --> 00:05:08.314
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:11.266
The poem blank be read as a protest.

72
00:05:11.566 --> 00:05:14.940
A, may well. B, is being. C, have to.

73
00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:20.569
The answer is A: may well.

74
00:05:21.717 --> 00:05:24.819
The meaning beneath what characters actually say is the blank.

75
00:05:25.119 --> 00:05:28.715
A, canon. B, subtext. C, stanza.

76
00:05:32.267 --> 00:05:34.255
The answer is B: subtext.

77
00:05:35.417 --> 00:05:37.370
Which sentence hedges correctly?

78
00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:44.702
A, The ending seems to be ironic.. B, The ending seems being ironic.. C, The ending seem to ironic..

79
00:05:48.267 --> 00:05:51.323
The answer is A: The ending seems to be ironic..

80
00:05:52.483 --> 00:05:56.387
The set of works widely seen as most important is the blank.

81
00:05:56.687 --> 00:06:00.085
A, abstract. B, lens. C, canon.

82
00:06:03.633 --> 00:06:05.923
The answer is C: canon.

83
00:06:07.083 --> 00:06:10.105
According to the reading, what may a feminist lens reveal?

84
00:06:10.405 --> 00:06:16.299
A, power a first reading misses. B, the author's address. C, the printing date.

85
00:06:19.833 --> 00:06:22.994
The answer is A: power a first reading misses.

86
00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:27.282
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:28.117 --> 00:06:31.312
How does a critical lens change the way we understand a story?

88
00:06:40.117 --> 00:06:41.733
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:42.033 --> 00:07:10.209
A critical lens makes us ask new questions. A historical lens may show how a story reflects its time, while a feminist lens may reveal who has power and who is silent. The same events can seem quite different from each angle. A good critic tends to combine several lenses, so that the interpretation is richer and less one-sided than a single first impression.

90
00:07:10.509 --> 00:07:12.184
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:13.083 --> 00:07:19.335
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and hedging: may well and seems to and tends to and arguably.

92
00:07:19.635 --> 00:07:24.307
Today's value is interpretive depth. Read slowly and look for more than one meaning.

93
00:07:24.607 --> 00:07:27.039
See you in Unit two. Goodbye!

94
00:07:27.339 --> 00:07:30.700
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

